<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <Description>Bobcat + TUnit — projects ordinary [Test] methods into the Bobcat model, so an existing suite renders as specifications without changing runners.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- TUnit.Core, not TUnit: the adapter needs the event-receiver interfaces and TestContext,
         and nothing from the engine or the assertion library. -->
    <PackageReference Include="TUnit.Core" />

    <!--
      DELIBERATELY NO Microsoft.Testing.Platform reference (issue #402).

      Bobcat.Xunit now ships an ITestSessionLifetimeHandler, which is what gives a projected suite
      a run bracket per RUN REQUEST instead of per process. This package does not follow, for three
      reasons:

      1. TUnit.Core depends on NO test platform at all — checked in its nuspec: Microsoft.CSharp,
         System.Collections.Immutable, System.Text.Json, System.Threading.Tasks.Extensions. That is
         exactly what makes this package safe to ship beside a pinned platform, and it is the
         property a platform reference here would spend. TUnit.Engine needs Microsoft.Testing
         .Platform 2.4.0 while src pins 1.9.1 (because that is what xunit.v3 3.2.2 builds against),
         so pulling the platform in here walks straight at the version collision that pin exists to
         avoid.

      2. There is nothing to serve yet. The per-request bracket exists for warm runs in the
         resident runner, and the projected lane is cold-only — the remaining work there needs
         decisions about package layering and a per-request command channel the protocol does not
         offer (see docs/warm-projected-runs.md). A TUnit suite has no warm path to be the first
         consumer of.

      3. Nothing regresses by not doing it. MarkerStepRun's ProcessExit backstop is still the
         bracket for any host with no session hook, so a TUnit suite behaves exactly as it did:
         one run per process, which for a one-shot `dotnet test` is the correct answer anyway.

      Revisit when a TUnit suite actually wants warm residency — and then the question is whether
      the platform reference belongs here or in a separate Bobcat.TUnit.Platform package, so that
      this one keeps the property described in (1).
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Bobcat\Bobcat.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
